  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Tuesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Conditions are quiet this morning across\nall of northern and central Nevada. High clouds are moving through\nthe area. There is some patchy freezing fog across extreme\nsouthwestern valleys in Humboldt county this morning. \n\nAll models are depicting the upper ridge that has been over the\narea the last several days will continue to reside within the\nvicinity. This will mean quiet weather conditions and warmer than\nnormal high temperatures. Readings will be in the 50s and 60s\nthrough roughly late Thursday. Overnight lows will be in the 20s\nand 30s. Winds will general remain the light side with a few\nafternoon gusts in the 20s.\n\nThere continues to be the potential for active weather across\nportions of northern and central Nevada beginning late Friday. \nModels have had difficulty handling the forecast scenario due to \nvariability in timing and strength of the aforementioned system. \nIt appears the upper ridge will be pushing east on Thursday with \nlow pressure beginning to impact the area late in the Friday \ntimeframe. Cooler weather along with periods of shower activity is\nexpected through roughly the end of this forecast time frame. \nCurrent long term will follow this trajectory with temperatures \ncooling to at or below normal levels during the weekend into early\nnext week.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Minor tweaks were made to the\nnear term grids to account for the patchy freezing fog seen \nacross extreme southwest Humboldt county this morning. Otherwise,\nno other changes were made to the NBM. Confidence remains high on\nthe ridge maintaining control of the weather for northern and \ncentral Nevada through roughly Thursday. Confidence is low to\nmoderate on the first system making its way into the area late\nFriday, with very low confidence pertaining to the weather after\nSaturday due to continued run to run variability.",
